0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": 11.79, "lean": 1.0, "environment": 7.9, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.89}, "rating_margin": 12.11, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.66, "baseline_margin": 11.98, "analyst_adjustment": 0.02, "key_factors": ["National Republicans withdrew support and pulled roughly $5 million from Oberacker's campaign", "Riley holds a large financial edge: $4.8M raised and $3.2M on hand versus $393K for Oberacker", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely D or better across nine raters", "District is only slightly Democratic-leaning and was decided by under three points in the last two cycles"], "rationale": "Riley benefits from incumbency, a dominant fundraising advantage, and a favorable midterm environment, while Oberacker lost national Republican backing in September. Ratings and markets align on a clear but not absolute Democratic edge, so Likely D fits better than Safe D given the district's recent narrow margins.", "watch": "A late infusion of outside Republican spending or a shift in the national midterm environment could bring the race back toward the narrow margins of the last two cycles.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Josh Riley (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Josh Riley defends a Hudson Valley district that has been decided by fewer than three points in each of the last two elections, though it leans slightly Democratic. He enters November with a decisive money advantage, having raised $4.8 million and holding $3.2 million on hand, while Republican Peter Oberacker has raised $1.2 million with under $400,000 available.\n\nThe race's trajectory changed in September, when national Republicans pulled roughly $5 million in planned spending and withdrew support from Oberacker, treating the seat as a triage target. Prediction markets price Riley's chances around 95 to 97 percent, and expert ratings range from Likely D to Safe D.